---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const roles = [
  { org: 'Nura', role: 'Founder and backend engineer on an app for the night after a fight', when: '2026–' },
  { org: 'Intosoft', role: 'Backend engineer on search, real-time, and billing', when: '2024–2025' },
  { org: 'Yatri Design Studio', role: 'Software engineer on EV charging admin and inventory', when: '2023–2024' },
  { org: 'Leapfrog Technology', role: 'Software engineer intern', when: '2022' },
]
---

<Base title="About" description="Bishal Hona, a backend engineer in Nepal with about three years of Node and TypeScript, currently building Nura, an app for the night after a fight with your partner.">
  <section class="shell ab-head">
    <p class="label">About</p>
    <h1 class="display ab-title measure">I'm Bishal, a backend engineer in Nepal.</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="shell ab-body">
    <div class="ab-prose measure flow">
      <p>
        I've spent about three years writing Node and TypeScript. For the last
        few months, most of that time has gone into Nura, an app for the night
        after a fight with your partner.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nura started in May as a journaling app with rituals and an insights
        screen. Building it taught me what the product needed to be. Someone
        who opens an app at 11pm, still upset, can't face a blank page. They
        need to be asked the right question, one at a time, and to leave with
        something they can keep. So I pulled 1,500 posts and 10,000 comments
        from relationship forums to hear how people actually describe those
        nights. Then I wrote the product down properly and rebuilt the backend
        from that spec.
      </p>
      <p>That rebuild is the clearest picture of how I work.</p>
      <p>
        <b>I start with the person on the other end.</b> In Nura's case, that's
        someone whose partner might pick up their phone. That one sentence
        shaped dozens of decisions. There's exactly one notification in the
        product, and its data type has no room for anyone's words. Deleting an
        account destroys that person's encryption key before anything else
        happens. Error reports and traces pass through a gate that drops
        anything it doesn't recognize, so a crash report can't carry someone's
        message out of the server.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>I think about what a decision costs and what it earns.</b> Screening
        every message for safety adds latency and cost to every turn. I took
        that on after measuring that the cheaper design could never catch more
        than 30% of what it needed to. Comparing two classifiers across 145
        test cases then cost 62 cents to run. The paywall shows at the moment
        someone keeps a card, and never in the middle of a hard conversation.
        A session that ended with crisis resources on screen never counts
        toward the free limit. Some of those are engineering calls and some
        are business calls, and I like being in the room for both.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>I care about the parts around the feature.</b> A job queue that keeps
        running when one job fails badly. A refresh token that signs out every
        device the moment a stolen one is reused. A background sweep that
        recovers lost work without ever sending the same notification twice.
        The feature someone asked for is rarely the hard part.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>I say what isn't done.</b> Nura's safety screen catches 97% of
        disclosures in testing. It still misses one kind of message that every
        engine I've tried misses, and the numbers are fitted on the same cases
        they're scored on. Both of those are written in the results, beside
        the headline. I'd rather someone trust a smaller number than question
        a bigger one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>I build with AI coding agents, and I write the rules they work
        under.</b> I design the system and make the product calls. The rules
        are types that make wrong states impossible to write, architecture
        boundaries the build enforces, and a test for every rule that I've
        watched fail by breaking the code on purpose. Every phase ends with a
        written log of what shipped, what changed, and what I chose to leave
        out and why. The agents type faster than I do. Deciding what to build,
        and what to refuse, is still my job.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm happiest on small teams building something that matters to the
        people using it, where the backend engineer is expected to have an
        opinion about the product.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ab-side">
      <p class="label ab-side__label">Background</p>
      <ul class="ab-timeline">
        {roles.map((r) => (
          <li class="ab-role">
            <span class="ab-role__when label">{r.when}</span>
            <span class="ab-role__org mono">{r.org}</span>
            <span class="ab-role__desc">{r.role}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <p class="label ab-side__label ab-side__label--2">Working with</p>
      <p class="ab-stack mono">
        Node · TypeScript · PostgreSQL · MongoDB · Redis · BullMQ · Socket.IO · Docker · AWS
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .ab-head { padding-top: clamp(2rem, 1.5rem + 3vw, 4rem); }
  .ab-title {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    margin-top: 0.8rem;
  }

  .ab-body {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.5rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 1.5rem + 4vw, 4.5rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.6fr 1fr;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 4vw, 5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .ab-prose p { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step-1); line-height: 1.65; color: var(--graphite); }
  .ab-prose b { color: var(--ink); }

  .ab-side { position: sticky; top: 2rem; }
  .ab-side__label { display: block; margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
  .ab-side__label--2 { margin-top: 2.4rem; }

  .ab-timeline {
    list-style: none; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .ab-role {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .ab-role:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .ab-role__when { color: var(--rust); }
  .ab-role__org { color: var(--ink); font-size: var(--step-0); }
  .ab-role__desc { color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.45; }

  .ab-stack {
    color: var(--graphite); font-size: var(--step--1); line-height: 1.9;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--ink); background: var(--panel);
  }

  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .ab-body { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .ab-side { position: static; }
  }
</style>
